---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import GoalBoard from '../components/GoalBoard.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Goal board — WAGademy"
  description="Write your session goals in verb + deliverable + size form and drag them from To do to Done. Saved in your browser."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Tool</p>
      <h1>Goal board</h1>
      <p>
        Three columns and a pile of stickies — the board from the room, in your browser.
        Write what you're doing in the next pomodoro, then drag it across when it's done.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap">
    <GoalBoard variant="full" />

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Writing goals that actually work</h2>
      <p>
        This is the part most people get wrong, and it is the difference between a productive
        session and a vague one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Vague goals fail invisibly.</strong> "Work on my thesis." "Read some papers."
        "Do analysis." At the end of the session you can't say whether you succeeded, so you learn
        nothing and feel bad anyway.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Specific goals are checkable.</strong> "Draft 300 words of the methods intro."
        "Read and take notes on three papers about X." "Code the data-cleaning step for dataset Y."
      </p>

      <blockquote><strong>VERB</strong> + <strong>DELIVERABLE</strong> + <strong>SIZE</strong></blockquote>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Verb</strong> — draft, code, plot, outline, revise, annotate</li>
        <li><strong>Deliverable</strong> — the thing a stranger could point at</li>
        <li><strong>Size</strong> — sized to fit one pomodoro of 45–50 minutes</li>
      </ul>

      <p><em>"Revise two paragraphs of discussion §4.2 using Tuesday's feedback."</em></p>

      <p>
        <strong>The stranger test:</strong> could someone who isn't you verify it got done?
        If not, sharpen it. Most first attempts fail this test — that's normal, and catching it
        now is the whole point.
      </p>

      <div class="callout do">
        <h3>Do this now — 5 minutes</h3>
        <p>
          Add three goals above, one per pomodoro, each in verb + deliverable + size form. Run each
          through the stranger test before you start the timer.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then decide your <strong>distraction plan</strong> — what you'll do when you're tempted to
          check email. "Write the thought down and keep going" works considerably better than "resist".
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Why moving them by hand matters</h2>
      <p>
        In the live sessions we run a shared board with these same three columns, one sticky per
        objective and a colour per person, dragged across as the session goes.
      </p>
      <p>
        It sounds trivial. It isn't: moving a sticky to <em>Done</em> is a small, real reward at the
        exact moment the work finishes, and over a series the board becomes a visible log of
        everything the group shipped. Working alone, a paper sticky on the edge of your screen does
        the same job. The requirement is only that the goals are <strong>visible and moved by
        hand</strong> — not buried in a task app you'll stop opening.
      </p>

      <h2>The report-back</h2>
      <p>
        At the end of a session, look at what's still sitting in <em>To do</em> and ask why —
        out loud, to someone, if there is someone. A missed goal is not a confession. It is evidence
        of how long your work actually takes, which is the single most useful thing a research
        student can learn, and the reason the next session's goals will be better than this one's.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>This board lives in your browser.</strong> Nothing is sent anywhere and nothing is
          shared — which also means it won't follow you to another device, and clearing your site
          data clears it. For a group, put this on the shared screen, or use real sticky notes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a class="btn" href="/timer/">Open the timer</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
